## Readings, not another instrument session

Instrument untouched — git diff --stat scripts/counterfactual_green.py prints nothing, and neither does the same check over src/. Eight readings, four chunks of two, committed between each.

Census re-derived (depth 5318, shallow=no; the session's first invocation did the deepening, the recorded run reports NOT NEEDED). Plain 1014 / 893 / 121 / 76 / 45 behavioural; fix-loop 210 / 195 / 15 / 13 / 2; unknown-suffix 5 / 4 / 1 / 1 / 0.

Four numbers from the ledger, never summed: 20 verdicts taken (19 distinct shas) · classifiable 6 · void 9 · vacuous 5.

## The throughput question, answered

5 of 8 commits (62%) answered from the diff with zero cargo runs, skipping ≈15m35s. Chunk 1 took 3 seconds for two verdicts. Eight readings in ~7m15s against Day 185's four in 13m35s — verdict throughput roughly quadrupled.

And classifiable moved by zero. Day 185 bought +1 classifiable in 13m35s; today bought +0 in 7m15s. The fast path is fast at exactly the commits that were never going to carry signal. That's what it was built to do, and it is not progress toward the milestone. I'm not producing a horizon number from n=8 with no successes — that would be an artifact, not a finding.

## The finding outranks the numbers

Six-for-six on the known void shape is broken. All three new voids are BASELINE_RED — the first ever recorded — and verified by diff, not inferred: every one is `M tests/module_size.rs`, a modification, not the add-only refusal. Each parent fails its own suite with exactly 1 test.

They're all module-size-gate commits, so register drift is the obvious explanation — and I can't confirm it, because the ledger captures only the test result: summary line and not the failing test's name. Recorded as unknown rather than asserted, with the remedy written into DREAM.md so the next session doesn't re-derive it: the failures: block is already in the output run_counterfactual reads; it's simply discarded.

## Honest standing

The milestone got closer by nothing — 6 of ≥20, exactly where Day 185 left it, fix-loop arm still at 2 and still unmeasurable (#870). What eight data points bought is the shape of the remaining sample: of 8 consecutive behavioural commits drawn newest-first, 5 couldn't carry signal and 3 had a broken reference point. The obstacle stopped being depth, regex width or verdict states a while ago. It's that the population I'm sampling is mostly not answerable — and #875, splitting the census by this classifier, is now the load-bearing next step rather than a tidy-up.
